//! flags: -p ssa

// `defer()` as an SSA rewrite (ssa::defer in SSA/Defer.cpp): a tail-recursive
// call becomes an entry on a queue, and the function that owns the queue
// drains it after the loop that filled it.
//
// `fill` sums n down to one into `out[i]`. Its recursive call is in tail
// position and the sum it carries is in its own arguments, so the call's
// continuation is exactly those arguments: `n` and `acc` change every step and
// `i` differs per producer iteration, so the three of them are the entry; `out`
// is the same array at every call and stays a parameter of the drain's call.
//
// What to read for: `fill` takes the queue and returns a flag, its recursive
// call is a push and `return true`, and `run` holds one queue -- a count and
// an array of entries sized by the parfor's trip count -- pushes into it from
// the parfor, and then loops before returning: while the queue is not empty,
// take its count, reset it, and run that many entries, each successor pushed
// at a slot at or before the one being read. The drain's parfor is named
// after the queue.
func fill(n : i32, acc : i32, out : mut array[i32, 8], i : i32) -> void {
  if (n <= 0) {
    out[i] = acc;
    return;
  }
  fill(n - 1, acc + n, out, i);
  return;
}

func run(out : mut array[i32, 8]) -> void {
  parfor i in 0:8 {
    fill(i, 0, out, i);
  }
  return;
}

schedule {
  sums = run.queue(root);
  fill.defer(fill, sums);
}
